--- MESSAGE ---
CHANGELOG: the entries the last two commits should have carried

The rule landed three commits ago: an entry is written with each commit's git-record, in
the same commit. The next two commits did not carry one. This adds them.

Worth recording plainly rather than quietly backfilling, because the failure is
informative about the rule itself. Both misses had a plausible-looking excuse at the
moment they happened. The release commit cut `[Unreleased]` into a version block, so
there was no obvious place to put an entry and the work it contained — the workflow's new
integrity checks — silently ended up recorded nowhere. The deprecation commit looked like
pure infrastructure, the kind the rule explicitly says may warrant no line.

Both readings were wrong on the same point: "a release will now refuse to publish a jar
whose version does not match its tag" is an observable fact about how this project ships,
which is exactly what the CHANGELOG is for. Infrastructure that changes what the project
guarantees is not internal.

The lesson is not that the trigger is wrong. Attaching the entry to the git-record is
still the right event — it is the step that reliably happens. The lesson is that a
trigger only fires if it is actually consulted, so the question at record-writing time has
to be asked out loud: what did this change, for someone who does not read commits? Two
commits after writing the rule was the cheapest possible place to learn that, and better
than the alternative, which was three months.
